.TH GIT\-TREE\-MERGED 1 "2026-08-20" "git\-tree\-merged" "Git Extras"
.SH NAME
git\-tree\-merged \- find a commit's tree on another branch
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git tree\-merged
.RI [ commit ]
.RB [ \-\-onto
.IR ref ]
.RB [ \-\-fetch ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.br
.B git tree\-merged \-\-self\-test
.SH DESCRIPTION
Squash, rebase and cherry\-pick all rewrite commit SHAs, so "is my work on
main?" cannot be answered by SHA or by
.BR "git branch \-\-merged" .
A tree hash is a content hash of the whole worktree, so comparing trees
answers it directly: equal tree means every file is byte\-identical, whatever
the history looks like.
.PP
.B git tree\-merged
resolves
.I commit
to its tree and walks
.I ref
newest\-first, stopping at the first commit with the same tree. It reports
that commit and its subject, or reports that no such commit exists.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I commit
Commit to look for. Defaults to
.BR HEAD .
.TP
.BI \-\-onto " ref"
Branch to search. Defaults to
.BR origin/main .
.TP
.B \-\-fetch
Refresh
.I ref
from its remote first. Requires network. A
.IR remote / branch
ref is fetched from that remote; a local ref is left alone.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
No output, exit code only.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage summary. Note that
.B git tree\-merged \-\-help
is intercepted by
.BR git (1)
and shows this manual page instead; use
.B git tree\-merged \-h
for the short usage.
.TP
.B \-\-self\-test
Build a throwaway repository, squash\-merge a branch into it, and assert both
verdicts. Exits 0 if the checks pass.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
A commit with the same tree was found on
.IR ref .
.TP
.B 1
No such commit.
.TP
.B 2
Usage or setup error, such as an unknown flag or an unresolvable ref.
.SH EXAMPLES
Is my current branch's content already on
.BR origin/main ?
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
git tree\-merged
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check a specific branch against a release branch, refreshing it first:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
git tree\-merged my\-feature \-\-onto origin/release\-1.2 \-\-fetch
.EE
.RE
.PP
Delete every local branch whose content has landed:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
git for\-each\-ref \-\-format='%(refname:short)' refs/heads |
while read \-r b; do
    git tree\-merged "$b" \-q && git branch \-D "$b"
done
.EE
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR git\-branch (1),
.BR git\-cherry (1),
.BR git\-log (1),
.BR git\-patch\-id (1)
